<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  /Library/LaunchDaemons/tierstore-fs.plist: `tierstore fs` as a LaunchDaemon on a Mac, the virtual
  build file system's NFSv3 frontend (docs/design/virtual-build-fs.md section 8). It serves the NFS
  and MOUNT protocols on loopback only (`fs.nfs.listen`), mounts the export read-only at
  `fs.mountpoint` as `fs.nfs.mount_user`, and unmounts on SIGTERM. root:wheel, mode 0644.

  The job runs as _tierstore, the user and group of tierstore.plist, because the fill socket of the
  store answers only a peer of its own user: a job of any other user, root included, has every fill
  refused. Nothing of it needs root: the NFS socket is on an unprivileged loopback port, and
  `mount_nfs`, `umount` and `diskutil unmount force` of a loopback export need none when the mount
  user owns the mount point. `fs.nfs.mount_user` is left out of the file (the user of the process
  is the default), and the mount point, `fs.mountpoint`, is a directory _tierstore owns, which the
  installer package makes. The file is /usr/local/tierstore/etc/fs.toml (packaging/macos/fs.toml
  is its example), which has the `[fs]` table and holds no secret and no key file (its directory is
  root:_tierstore, mode 0750); no environment variable is set, the process reads none, and launchd
  needs no notification of readiness.

  The program is the binary the installer package lays down,
  /Library/Application Support/tierstore/bin/tierstore, the same binary as tierstore.plist's. The
  installer package (`cargo xtask release macos-pkg`) installs this file. It is
  packaging/hostkeeper/units/tierstore-fs.plist with one difference, the program, which that file
  has at /usr/local/libexec/tierstore/tierstore. Nothing loads the job but the update hook
  (`tierstore update-hook`, param fs_launchd_label). A restart of the process leaves a gap in
  serving: the mount is hard, its clients wait, and the restarted process finds the mount, reopens
  its table of tree numbers and serves on the same port.

  The mount is hard with the mount option deadtimeout: a client waits for a server that is only
  restarting, and gives up on one that has been gone for the whole of that time. launchd sends
  SIGTERM on bootout. The process unmounts first: `umount`, then `diskutil unmount force`, then its
  own unmount(2) with MNT_FORCE, each bounded by `fs.nfs.unmount_wait`, and exits 0 once the mount
  is gone. If all three fail it keeps serving and logs why, and launchd kills it at ExitTimeOut;
  the mount option deadtimeout then ends the mount that has no server. ExitTimeOut (150 s) is more
  than the unmount takes at its longest: four steps of at most 25 s each and 5 s to collect a killed
  command, 120 s, so launchd never kills the process in the middle of an unmount. AbandonProcessGroup
  is not set, so launchd's kill of the job also kills a helper command the process left. KeepAlive
  starts the process again whenever it exits while the job is loaded, at most once in
  ThrottleInterval (10 s).

  crates/tierstore/tests/packaging_macos.rs holds this file to the other plist and the binary to
  its command line.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>tierstore-fs</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Library/Application Support/tierstore/bin/tierstore</string>
    <string>fs</string>
    <string>--config</string>
    <string>/usr/local/tierstore/etc/fs.toml</string>
  </array>
  <key>UserName</key>
  <string>_tierstore</string>
  <key>GroupName</key>
  <string>_tierstore</string>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
  <integer>150</integer>
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Standard</string>
  <key>Umask</key>
  <integer>63</integer>
  <!-- Jobs' overlays keep files of the mount open as long as a job runs; the limit bounds the distinct cache files held open, which the process shares across handles. -->
  <key>SoftResourceLimits</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
    <integer>1048576</integer>
  </dict>
  <key>HardResourceLimits</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
    <integer>1048576</integer>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/usr/local/tierstore/log/tierstore-fs.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/usr/local/tierstore/log/tierstore-fs.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
